<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">5 October in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 5 October, from 610 to 1962.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heraclius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">610 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,416 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Heraclius is crowned Byzantine emperor</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Having reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 3 October at the head of the revolt against the unpopular emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocas">Phocas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> was crowned in the Chapel of St Stephen within the Great Palace. He reigned until 641.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">816 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,210 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pope Stephen IV crowns Louis the Pious emperor at Reims</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>, co-emperor with his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> since 813 and sole ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> since 814, received an imperial coronation from Pope Stephen IV at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>. The next year he settled his succession in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordinatio-imperii">Ordinatio imperii</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordinatio-imperii">Ordinatio imperii</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/869">869 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,157 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Fourth Council of Constantinople opens</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A church council opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> that deposed the patriarch Photius. Counted by the Catholic Church as the eighth ecumenical council but rejected by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, it foreshadowed the later schism between East and West.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1143 CE <span class="tih-ago">883 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Alfonso VII recognises Afonso Henriques as king of Portugal</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zamora">Treaty of Zamora</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII of León and Castile</a> acknowledged his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso Henriques</a> as king of Portugal, in the presence of the papal legate. The treaty is regarded as the founding act of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zamora">Treaty of Zamora</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII of León and Castile</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1762">1762 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">264 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Gluck's Orfeo ed Euridice premieres in Vienna</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christoph-willibald-gluck">Christoph Willibald Gluck</a>'s Orfeo ed Euridice was first performed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgtheater">Burgtheater</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Its simplicity and dramatic directness began Gluck's reform of opera away from showy vocal display.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1789">1789 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">237 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Parisian women march on Versailles</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Thousands of Parisians, led by market women angry at the price of bread, marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>. The next day they brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> and his family back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, placing the monarchy under the eye of the Revolution.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1962">1962 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">64 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Dr. No premieres, launching the James Bond films</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Dr. No, starring Sean Connery as Ian Fleming's secret agent, premiered at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Pavilion. It began one of the longest-running series in cinema history.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1962">1962 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">64 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Beatles release their first single, 'Love Me Do'</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Parlophone released 'Love Me Do', the Beatles' debut single in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. It reached only number 17, but within a year the band from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> had become a national sensation.</p></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 5 October <span class="n">3</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-of-trebizond"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alexios-iii-of-trebizond.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Alexios III of Trebizond</span><span class="tp">1338–1390</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/francis-william-reitz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/francis-william-reitz.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Francis William Reitz</span><span class="tp">1844–1934</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/vaclav-havel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vaclav-havel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Václav Havel</span><span class="tp">1936–2011</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 5 October <span class="n">7</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/justin-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Justin II</span><span class="tp">c. 520–578</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/henry-iii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor</span><span class="tp">1016–1056</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-france"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/philip-iii-of-france.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Philip III of France</span><span class="tp">1245–1285</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/kaibara-ekken"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kaibara-ekken.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Kaibara Ekken</span><span class="tp">1630–1714</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-cornwallis-1st-marquess-cornwallis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/charles-cornwallis-1st-marquess-cornwallis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Charles Cornwallis, 1st Marquess Cornwallis</span><span class="tp">1738–1805</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tecumseh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Tecumseh</span><span class="tp">c. 1768–1813</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-yudenich"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nikolai-yudenich.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Nikolai Yudenich</span><span class="tp">1862–1933</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
